 `find.tau()` gives the period of the regimen that `find.dose.regimen()` finds, using the segment with the most doses.  The dose times are sorted and the ones that repeat are dropped.  If the spacings between consecutive doses are all the same, that spacing is `tau`.  Otherwise a cycle of two to four doses per interval is looked for:  each dose's time within the interval falls into one of the positions of the cycle, the spacings between the positions differ from one another, every position holds doses from at least two complete intervals, and the positions fit the doses clearly better than evenly spaced doses would, so that doses recorded a few hours early or late are not mistaken for a cycle.  Next, spacings that are all within a tolerance of their median are doses given evenly with some scatter in the recorded times, and the median spacing is `tau`.  Otherwise runs of the same spacing form the regimen.  A gap between runs that is a whole number of the spacing is read as a missed dose, a dose between two gaps that fit neither is a dose off schedule, and doses squeezed into one interval are extra doses; each gives a `pknca_warning_tau_irregular_dosing` warning naming the doses.  Runs with different spacings are a change of regimen, which gives a `pknca_warning_tau_regimen_change` warning.  When the runs need missed doses or a change of regimen to explain the doses, a cycle that most doses follow is used instead if it explains them at least as well.  If none of that fits, no dosing interval is reported.
 
-Times are compared within a tolerance, so a dose recorded at 23.6 hours is a daily dose.  The intervals found are matched to the `tau.choices` option's values when it is given.  When it is `NA` (the default) and the time unit is given with the `timeu` argument, they are matched to the usual nominal intervals, from every 4 hours to every 12 weeks.  An interval that matches none of them gives a `pknca_warning_tau_not_nominal` warning.  Without either, the unit of the times is not known and the interval is the one found in the data.
+Times are compared within a tolerance, so a dose recorded at 23.6 hours is a daily dose.  The intervals found are matched to the `tau.choices` option's values when it is given.  When it is `NA` (the default) and the time unit is known, they are matched to the usual nominal intervals, from every 4 hours to every 12 weeks.  `PKNCAdata()` and `pk.nca()` know the time unit when it is given to `PKNCAconc()` as `timeu`; `find.tau()` and `choose.auc.intervals()` take it as their `timeu` argument.  An interval that matches none of them gives a `pknca_warning_tau_not_nominal` warning.  Without either, the unit of the times is not known and the interval is the one found in the data.
 
 Looking for a repeating pattern first is what keeps a regimen with a regular gap in it from being misread.  Dosing three times a day at 0, 6, and 12 hours repeats daily; the 12 hour overnight gap is the regimen, not two doses that were never given.  That holds when a dose is missing too:  the doses that were given keep their time of day, so the regimen is still three times daily, with one missed dose.
 
